WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.480
Calcium is the same ion in every cell that uses it.

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.780
So the message must lie in where it rises, and when.

3
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:19.180
Buffer proteins capture a free calcium ion quickly, so calcium does not travel far

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.840
from its channel. A protein beside the channel sees a high level.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.000
A protein across the cell sees nothing.

6
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:32.963
Under a steady stimulus, many cells do not hold calcium high.

7
00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:35.703
They spike again and again.

8
00:00:36.883 --> 00:00:45.783
In 1986, Woods, Cuthbertson and Cobbold injected single rat liver cells with a light-emitting calcium reporter.

10
00:00:46.583 --> 00:00:50.283
The more hormone they gave, the more often the spikes came.

11
00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:55.683
The dose was written in frequency, the way FM radio carries sound.

12
00:00:56.617 --> 00:01:01.937
Something has to count the spikes, and CaM kinase II can.

13
00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:06.417
Its twelve subunits are assembled around a central hub.

14
00:01:07.397 --> 00:01:12.657
When two neighbours hold calmodulin at the same moment, one phosphorylates the

15
00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:17.677
other. The phosphorylated subunit stays active after calcium falls.

16
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.600
Spikes that come close together build on each other, because subunits are still

17
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.120
loaded from the last one.

18
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.580
Spikes far apart each start from nothing.

19
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:36.920
In 1998, De Koninck and Schulman showed with purified enzyme that the lasting

20
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.280
activity of CaM kinase II depends on the frequency of calcium pulses.

21
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.540
Place and timing carry the message.
